  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The deliberate poisoning of all fourteen wells and the burning of the communal grain stores constitute a violation of the prohibition on attacking objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population.",
      "reasoning": "Under the core principles of international humanitarian law, objects such as water sources and food stores that are essential for civilian survival are protected from attack unless they are being used for military purposes. The wells and grain stores in the Velmir Valley were not used by Drennick forces for any military purpose and were the sole source of drinking water and winter food for 6,000 civilians. Their destruction therefore breaches the rule that forbids making such objects useless for the civilian population.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The destruction cannot be justified by the doctrine of imperative military necessity because the wells and grain stores were not being used to support Drennick’s military operations.",
      "reasoning": "The principle of military necessity permits attacks only when they are directed at a legitimate military objective and are required to achieve a concrete military advantage. Here, the occupying forces relied on their own logistics and did not depend on the wells or grain stores for any operational need. Consequently, there was no concrete military advantage to be gained by destroying these objects, rendering the claimed necessity unfounded.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The order to ‘fill or poison all wells and burn all grain stores’ violates the principle of distinction, which obliges parties to differentiate between military objectives and civilian objects.",
      "reasoning": "Distinction requires that attacks be directed solely at military objectives. The wells and grain stores are civilian objects; they were not being used for any military purpose and were essential for the civilian population’s survival. Issuing an order to destroy them indiscriminately demonstrates a failure to make the required distinction, thereby breaching this fundamental rule of IHL.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The brigade commander bears individual criminal responsibility under the doctrine of command responsibility for ordering the unlawful destruction of civilian objects.",
      "reasoning": "Command responsibility imposes liability on superiors who knew or should have known about the commission of war crimes by subordinates and failed to prevent or punish such acts. The commander issued a written order explicitly directing the poisoning of wells and burning of grain stores, fully aware that these actions would deprive civilians of essential resources. His direct involvement satisfies the knowledge and control elements required for command responsibility.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
